rom the pointers, applying the same four FP32 MOPA operations in the same order, then advancing each pointer by `n`. Handle `k=n-1` in a separate final block with no pointer increments. Keep the existing predicates, ZA selectors, output stores, and transpose unchanged. This removes the per-step `k+1<n` branch while avoiding pointer formation beyond the matrix allocation; for `n=1`, only the final block runs.", "risks": "The compiler may already remove or predict the conditional increment, so this may not improve performance. The extra final block may affect code size or scheduling. Confirm pointers address the same reduction rows for full and partial tiles, including odd dimensions; check n=0 and n=1, all supplied correctness sizes and input kinds, and the fixed 0.002 tolerance. Compare repeated trials with fresh paired AC controls under the unchanged benchmark contract. Results are unverified until measured; noisy or joint outcomes cannot establish causality."}, {"experiment_id": "compiler_codegen", "recommendation": "Propose a source-level compiler experiment in the SME reduction loop: hoist the four repeated vector loads out of the four MOPA calls by keeping each loaded A and B vector in a named local, then issue the existing MOPA operations in the same selector order. The supplied source already expresses this pattern, so first inspect Apple clang 21 assembly for the incumbent and verify whether it emits redundant vector loads or spills around ZA operations. If it does, make only the smallest source change that addresses the observed code; otherwise abstain from a source change. Hypothesis: reducing redundant loads or spills could improve the reduction loop without changing FP32 arithmetic order, ZA selectors, ABI, or tail handling.", "risks": "No compiler output or measurements were supplied, so there is no evidence that the compiler emits redundant loads or spills; the source already reuses named vectors. A rewrite may compile identically or increase register 